# .trivyignore — residual CVEs in the BUNDLED scanner CLI binaries only.
#
# Scope and honesty notes (read before adding anything here):
#
# * Every entry below is a Go-dependency CVE compiled INTO one of the vendored
#   scanner CLIs that AK images ship and invoke:
#     - /usr/local/bin/grype  (upstream tag v0.118.0, BUILT FROM SOURCE in
#       docker/Dockerfile.backend{,.alpine} since #3352 — backend images)
#     - /usr/local/bin/trivy  (ghcr.io/artifact-keeper/trivy, pinned by digest
#       in docker/Dockerfile.scanner-adapter — scanner-adapter image only; the
#       backend images do NOT bundle trivy)
#   Both are short-lived CLI subprocesses the services shell out to. They are
#   NOT part of the network-reachable service surface (axum HTTP :8080 / gRPC
#   :9090 / adapter HTTP :8080). The vulnerable code paths (docker client
#   libs, OCI registry clients, sigstore verification, etc.) are not exercised
#   by how AK drives the tools.
#
# * The trivy CLI is NO LONGER bundled in the backend runtime images (issue
#   #1544): the backend drives Trivy via the sidecar (TRIVY_URL) and the
#   scanner-adapter (TRIVY_ADAPTER_URL). The only image that ships a trivy
#   binary is the scanner-adapter.
#
# * These are deliberately NOT suppressed in the backend service binary, the
#   scanner-adapter binary (rebuilt from source with a patched Go toolchain on
#   every build), or in the UBI/Alpine base OS packages. Only
#   bundled-scanner-binary CVEs are listed, and since #3352 the bar for
#   listing one is no longer "no fixed *tool release* exists" — both binaries
#   are now built by us, so that is nearly always true and stopped
#   discriminating. The bar is "no fixed version we can BUILD AGAINST exists".
#
# * NEITHER scanner binary comes from an upstream release stream any more.
#   trivy has been our own build of the pinned upstream tag since #3123
#   (ghcr.io/artifact-keeper/trivy, dependency overrides declared in that
#   repo's overrides.yaml). grype joined it at #3352: the backend Dockerfiles
#   now COMPILE upstream v0.118.0 from source with a Go toolchain floored at
#   go1.27.1, instead of copying the prebuilt binary out of
#   ghcr.io/anchore/grype.
#
#   The consequence for this file is the point of both changes. A Go STDLIB
#   CVE is now FIXABLE by us — it is a property of the compiler, and the fix
#   is a one-line GO_MIN_PATCH bump in the Dockerfiles. It must NOT be added
#   here. Likewise a trivy Go-dependency CVE with a fixed dependency version
#   belongs in that repo's overrides.yaml, not here. What is left for this
#   file is the genuinely unfixable: a dependency CVE where no consumable
#   fixed version exists to build against.
#
#   Before #3352 the argument for suppressing grype stdlib CVEs was that no
#   rebuilt grype release existed, which was true and is now irrelevant — we
#   are the ones doing the rebuilding. All seven stdlib tokens this file
#   carried were retired on that basis (tombstones at the bottom). Measured
#   with trivy 0.69.3 on 2026-08-16, rebuilt vs ghcr.io/anchore/grype:v0.117.0:
#   13 stdlib findings disappear and nothing new appears — HIGH+fixed 12 -> 1,
#   all severities 21 -> 8. The six that were failing Security Scan
#   (CVE-2026-33818/-56853/-56858/-56859/-56860/-56862) were never in this
#   file and deliberately never got here.
#
# * For the CVEs below the fix either does not exist upstream at all, or
#   exists only in a version that cannot be built against (see the
#   docker/docker note). Re-evaluate this whole file on every scanner bump.
#
# * DELETING an entry (#3309): scripts/ci/release-preflight.sh check 1 is a
#   HARD gate requiring every suppression on every active release/* branch to
#   be ACCOUNTED FOR here — either as a live token, or as an explicit
#   `# RETIRED: <token>` tombstone line (see the block at the bottom of this
#   file). To delete a suppression that a release branch still lists, first
#   measure that the finding is absent from main's shipped images, then
#   replace the token line with a tombstone. A tombstone is machine-read by
#   the gate: it records that main removed the token DELIBERATELY, as opposed
#   to never having received it (the #3039 forward-port miss, which the gate
#   still hard-fails). Never tombstone a token that is still being suppressed
#   — the gate fails a token listed both live and retired. Drop tombstones
#   entirely once no active release branch lists their token.
#
# * The CI publish GATE scans with `ignore-unfixed: true` and
#   `severity: CRITICAL,HIGH`, so the genuinely no-fix-available and
#   MEDIUM/LOW entries below do not block a publish; they are listed for
#   completeness so future maintainers see the full residual set in one place.
#
#   Read that as of #3122 only. BEFORE #3122 the gate discarded its severity
#   filter (trivy-action unsets TRIVY_SEVERITY for SARIF output) and failed on
#   a fixable CVE of ANY severity, so "MEDIUM/LOW doesn't gate" was NOT true
#   for most of this file's history — which is why entries accumulated here
#   that were really just a way to unblock a publish. Anything below justified
#   solely as "MEDIUM/LOW so it doesn't alert anyway" is now inert for gating
#   and should be re-evaluated on the next scanner bump rather than inherited.
#   A separate, non-blocking SARIF pass still reports ALL severities to the
#   code-scanning Security tab, so an entry removed from here becomes visible
#   there without gating.
#
# Grype tracker: https://github.com/anchore/grype/releases
# Trivy tracker: https://github.com/artifact-keeper/trivy — overrides.yaml is
#   the live record of what we diverge from upstream on, and its
#   upstream-watch workflow tracks new Aqua releases.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO grype Go-stdlib block any more (#3352).
#
# There used to be one here, listing CVE-2026-27145 / -39821 / -39822 / -42504
# / -42505 / -42507 and, separately, -46600, all justified as "grype v0.117.0
# is built with go1.26.3 and Anchore has shipped no rebuilt release, so there
# is no tool version to bump to". That was accurate and it was still the wrong
# shape of answer: it made every new Go stdlib advisory a release blocker
# resolved by editing this file. On 2026-08-16 six MORE stdlib CVEs
# (CVE-2026-33818 encoding/asn1, -56853 net/http, -56858 html/template,
# -56859 encoding/xml, -56860 net/url, -56862 crypto/tls) failed Security Scan
# on main and release/1.7.x for five consecutive runs, blocking all image
# publication and both the v1.7.5 and v1.8.0 tags.
#
# The Dockerfiles now build grype from source on a GO_MIN_PATCH-floored
# toolchain (go1.26.6 at #3352, go1.27.1 since #3655 moved the floor onto the
# minor the FROM line actually names), so the entire class is fixed rather
# than suppressed, and the seven tokens it
# listed are tombstoned at the bottom of this file. The next Go stdlib advisory
# is a GO_MIN_PATCH bump in docker/Dockerfile.backend{,.alpine} — NOT a new
# entry here. Adding one would re-create exactly the treadmill #3352 removed.
#
# The runtime-scope argument that block carried (GRYPE_DB_AUTO_UPDATE=false and
# GRYPE_CHECK_FOR_APP_UPDATE=false against a DB pre-seeded at build time, #1001,
# so grype makes no outbound HTTP at runtime) is still true and still not a
# fix. It is deliberately not reproduced as a justification, because it is no
# longer load-bearing: the vulnerable code is not linked in at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# grype v0.118.0 binary — github.com/docker/docker @ v28.5.2+incompatible
# CVE-2026-41567 / -41568 / -42306: NO upstream-fixed docker release exists yet
# (FixedVersion=none). CVE-2026-33997 / -34040 are fixed in docker 29.3.1 but
# grype v0.118.0 requires v28.5.2. When these were added (2026-08-16, trivy
# 0.69.3) CVE-2026-34040 was HIGH+fixed and therefore gating; re-measured
# 2026-09-03 with trivy 0.74.0 against the v0.118.0 binary, NO docker/docker
# token produces a finding at all (-41567/-42306 HIGH but unfixed, -33997 and
# -41568 MEDIUM, -34040 absent from the DB). The tokens stay live rather than
# being tombstoned because that absence is a DB-side change, not an
# artifact-side one, and a later DB revision can re-score any of them; the
# ledger convention below asks for a measured artifact-side basis. Code path: grype's Docker engine client for
# `docker:` image sources; the backend invokes grype against on-disk
# artifacts/SBOMs, not the Docker daemon.
#
# These are the ONE case that survives #3352, and they survive it for a
# measured reason rather than by inheritance. Building from source makes a
# dependency override possible in principle — it is what artifact-keeper/trivy
# does — but there is nothing to override to: with the grype source tree,
# `go mod edit -require=github.com/docker/docker@v29.3.1+incompatible` fails
# module resolution ("unknown revision v29.3.1", first tried on v0.117.0 on
# 2026-08-16), so the "fixed in 29.3.1" the DB reports is not a version this
# module path can resolve. Suppress when there is no fix to build against;
# rebuild when there is. Retry the override on the next grype bump.
#
# Retried on the v0.117.0 -> v0.118.0 bump (2026-09-03, #3655), which is what
# that instruction asks for. v0.118.0's go.mod still pins
# github.com/docker/docker v28.5.2+incompatible, unchanged from v0.117.0, and
# the same `go mod edit -require` still fails with the same "unknown revision
# v29.3.1". So all five tokens below stay LIVE and nothing is tombstoned for
# them. The same bump did retire the golang.org/x/mod override in the
# Dockerfiles (v0.118.0 pins x/mod v0.40.0 itself) and added a
# google.golang.org/grpc override for CVE-2026-84304 (HIGH, fixed in v1.83.1),
# and neither of those ever appeared in this file — which is the rule working:
# a dependency CVE with a version to build against gets BUILT, not listed.
CVE-2026-33997
CVE-2026-34040
CVE-2026-41567
CVE-2026-41568
CVE-2026-42306

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT SUPPRESSED (#3262) — go-git in the PUBLISHED images only.
#
# The 2026-08-10 weekly scan reported two findings against
# ghcr.io/.../artifact-keeper-backend:latest, both at usr/local/bin/grype:
#
#   CVE-2026-71556  HIGH    github.com/go-git/go-git/v5 v5.19.1 -> 5.19.2
#   CVE-2026-71557  MEDIUM  github.com/go-git/go-git/v5 v5.19.1 -> 5.19.2
#
# Neither belongs in this file. Measured 2026-08-15 with trivy 0.69.3,
# --ignore-unfixed:
#
#   * grype v0.117.0 — the upstream tag BOTH backend Dockerfiles built from
#     when this was measured — did not contain the go-git package at all. No
#     finding. Still true of the from-source build (#3352): same source tag,
#     re-measured 2026-08-16. No longer true as of v0.118.0 (2026-09-03): the
#     binary now links go-git v5.19.2, which is the FIXED version, so still no
#     finding — but the reason changed from "absent" to "patched".
#   * artifact-keeper-backend:latest is v1.6.3, built 2026-07-24, and
#     :dev was built 2026-08-07. Both predate the v0.116.0 -> v0.117.0 bump
#     (#3307), so both still carry the older grype that vendored go-git
#     v5.19.1 (and grpc-go v1.80.0, which is why GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf still
#     shows against those images while being correctly tombstoned below for
#     main).
#
# So these findings are already fixed on main and clear on the next publish.
# Suppressing them would record an exception for a build we no longer produce
# — the precise failure mode this file's header warns about. If they ever
# appear against a backend image whose version label matches current main,
# that is a real regression: investigate, do not add a line here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ===========================================================================
# RETIRED tombstones (#3309) — deliberate removals, machine-read by
# scripts/ci/release-preflight.sh check 1.
#
# Each line below records a token main has REMOVED because the finding was
# measured absent from every image main ships. The tokens still appear in
# release/1.5.x and/or release/1.6.x's .trivyignore; without these tombstones
# the pre-tag gate would (rightly) refuse the removal as a possible
# forward-port miss. A tombstone is NOT an active exception — nothing is
# suppressed by it. Delete a tombstone once no active release branch lists
# its token.
#
# Measurement record (2026-08-13, #3307):
# * GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf — suppressed while grype v0.116.0 shipped grpc-go
#   v1.80.0; grype v0.117.0 ships grpc-go v1.82.1 (the fixed version) and a
#   scan of ghcr.io/anchore/grype:v0.117.0 reports it at no severity.
# * The seven trivy tokens — all written against ghcr.io/aquasecurity/
#   trivy:0.72.0, which the scanner-adapter stopped shipping at #3123. The
#   replacement (ghcr.io/artifact-keeper/trivy, pinned by digest in
#   docker/Dockerfile.scanner-adapter) was scanned with no ignorefile at all
#   severities: none of them appears. Different base OS, different Go
#   toolchain, newer upstream tag, oras-go already overridden to 2.6.2.
#   (CVE-2026-46600 from that block was NOT retired then: it was re-measured on
#   2026-08-14 as live in the grype v0.117.0 binary's Go stdlib. #3352 retired
#   it below, on the second measurement record.)
#
# Measurement record (2026-08-16, #3352):
# * The seven grype Go-stdlib tokens — CVE-2026-27145 / -39821 / -39822 /
#   -42504 / -42505 / -42507 / -46600. All were suppressed because the binary
#   was compiled with go1.26.3 and no rebuilt grype release existed. The
#   backend Dockerfiles now compile upstream v0.117.0 themselves on a
#   go1.26.6-floored toolchain, so the stdlib they were reported against is no
#   longer shipped.
#
#   Measured with trivy 0.69.3 against the rebuilt binary, all severities, no
#   ignorefile: none of the seven appears, and neither do the six unsuppressed
#   siblings that were failing Security Scan (CVE-2026-33818/-56853/-56858/
#   -56859/-56860/-56862). Total findings against the grype binary go 21 -> 8
#   at all severities and 12 -> 1 at HIGH+fixed, with ZERO findings added —
#   the whole delta is stdlib, and the surviving 8 are the docker/docker and
#   x/mod/x/crypto entries the upstream binary already had.
#
#   These seven are the reason to read the header before adding a stdlib token
#   back: the class is now fixed at the source, and a new one belongs in
#   GO_MIN_PATCH, not here.
# ===========================================================================
# RETIRED: GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf (2026-08-13, grype grpc-go fixed in v0.117.0, #3307/#3309)
